            "question_number": 2,
            "total_questions": 10,
            "question": {
              "question": {
                "content": "A large freshwater system initially contains $L$ million liters of water in its surface reservoirs (lakes and rivers). Due to glacial melt, $M$ million liters of water are added to the region's overall freshwater supply *each year*. However, only $P$ percent of this added meltwater actually flows into the surface reservoirs; the rest infiltrates groundwater or evaporates. At the same time, $E$ million liters of water evaporate directly from the surface reservoirs *each year*.\n\nWrite an expression representing the total amount of water (in million liters) in the surface reservoirs after $Y$ years.\n[[\u2603 expression 1]]",
                "images": {},
                "widgets": {
                  "expression 1": {
                    "type": "expression",
                    "options": {
                      "buttonsVisible": "never",
                      "functions": [
                        "f",
                        "g",
                        "h"
                      ],
                      "times": false,
                      "answerForms": [
                        {
                          "value": "L + \\frac{MPY}{100} - EY",
                          "form": true,
                          "simplify": false,
                          "considered": "correct"
                        }
                      ],
                      "buttonSets": [
                        "basic"
                      ]
                    },
                    "graded": true,
                    "version": {
                      "major": 2,
                      "minor": 0
                    },
                    "alignment": "default",
                    "static": false
                  }
                }
              },
              "answerArea": {
                "calculator": false,
                "type": "multiple"
              },
              "hints": [
                {
                  "content": "Consider the initial amount of water. How does the water in the surface reservoirs change each year due to new water flowing in and water evaporating out? What mathematical operations represent these changes?",
                  "images": {},
                  "replace": false,
                  "widgets": {}
                },
                {
                  "content": "First, identify the initial amount of water. Then, calculate the amount of water added to the reservoirs annually from glacial melt, remembering that only $P$ percent of $M$ reaches them. Next, determine the amount of water lost from the reservoirs annually due to evaporation. Finally, combine these annual changes and multiply by the number of years, $Y$, to find the total change over time.",
                  "images": {},
                  "replace": false,
                  "widgets": {}
                },
                {
                  "content": "The initial amount of water in the surface reservoirs is $L$. Each year, $M$ million liters are added to the region's supply, but only $P$ percent of this flows into the reservoirs. So, the amount added to reservoirs annually is $M \\times \\frac{P}{100}$. Each year, $E$ million liters evaporate directly from the reservoirs. Therefore, the net change in water per year is $(M \\times \\frac{P}{100}) - E$. To find the total amount of water after $Y$ years, you will add this annual net change multiplied by $Y$ to the initial amount $L$. This gives $L + Y \\times (M \\times \\frac{P}{100} - E)$.",
                  "images": {},
                  "replace": false,
                  "widgets": {}
                }
              ],
